 ### Global Generator
-Maps a single global latent code $z$ to a 2D grid of local latent codes $W$ representing the spatial layout of the scene [S1]. It uses a constant input and successive modulated convolutional layers conditioned on $z$ [S1]. Upsampling is achieved via transposed convolution (stride 2) followed by bilinear filtering [S1]. The output resolution is typically $32 \times 32$, controlling the spatial region per local latent code [S1]. Choose this when explicit spatial layout of latent codes is required [S1].
+Maps a single global latent code $z$ to a 2D grid of local latent codes $W$ representing the spatial layout of the scene [S1]. It uses a constant input and successive modulated convolutional layers conditioned on $z$, upsampling resolution by $2\times$ via transposed convolution and bilinear filtering [S1]. Choose this when local spatial variations of the latent code are required [S1]. The output resolution is typically $32 \times 32$ [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Complexity:** The Mapping Network is $O(d \cdot 128)$ per sample. The Global Generator is significantly more expensive due to convolutional upsampling, with computational cost increasing with the output resolution ($32 \times 32$) [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Using feature maps directly from the radiance field (via global conditioning) produces higher quality results than sampling at low resolution and refining, but at a significantly higher computational cost [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Encodes whole sample information in one vector, enabling flexible instance-specific generation [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** High computational cost for the Global Generator variant [S1].
+*   **Complexity:** The Mapping Network is $O(d^2)$ per sample where $d$ is the latent dimension, with negligible memory overhead. The Global Generator is $O(H \cdot W \cdot C^2)$ in memory and compute, where $H, W$ are output resolutions and $C$ is channels [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Using feature maps directly from the radiance field produced higher quality results compared to sampling at low resolution and applying refinement blocks, but the computational cost is significantly higher [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Captures global semantic information efficiently [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** A single code may struggle with high-frequency local details without the Global Generator variant [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Originating from works on unconstrained scene generation and locally conditioned radiance fields, this method adapts techniques from StyleGAN [S1]. The concept of global vs. local conditioning distinguishes between encoding all sample information in one vector (global) versus spreading it over multiple codes assigned to spatio-temporal coordinates (local) [S2, unofficial].
+Global conditioning originates from the application of neural fields to unconstrained scene generation [S1]. The mapping network architecture follows StyleGAN principles [S1][S2, unofficial], specifically using a normalization step followed by linear layers with LeakyReLU activations [S1]. The concept of encoding the whole sample information in one latent vector is contrasted with local conditioning in the broader context of neural fields [S2, unofficial].
 
